      en: "The Independence Memorial Museum in Windhoek is a prominent historical monument dedicated to the struggle for Namibia's self-determination and liberation from colonial rule. Inaugurated on March 21, 2014, the 24th anniversary of Namibian independence, the museum stands on a site formerly occupied by a German colonial fortification. The striking 40-meter-tall triangular glass structure was designed and built by a North Korean architectural firm, reflecting a monumental socialist realism style. Inside, visitors can explore three floors of exhibits that chronicle the long path to freedom, including the resistance against German genocide and the later war against South African apartheid forces. The museum's exterior is flanked by two significant statues: one of the country's founding president, Sam Nujoma, and the Genocide Memorial, which honors the victims of the 1904–1908 conflict. From its upper floors, the building offers a panoramic view of the capital city, symbolizing the new era of Namibian sovereignty and national pride.",
